{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-73853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}yz+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2}$ at the point $(1,-1,1,-1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2})=2xyz+{y}^{2}z+y{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2})={x}^{2}z+2xyz+x{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2})={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}+2xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1) & =2(1)(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}(1)+(-1)(1)^{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1) & =(1)^{2}(1)+2(1)(-1)(1)+(1)(1)^{2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) & =(1)^{2}(-1)+(1)(-1)^{2}+2(1)(-1)(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-(-1) & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,-1,1)+(y-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,-1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,-1,1) \\\\ w+1 & =-2(x-1)+0\\cdot (y+1)-2(z-1) \\\\ w+1 & =-2x-2z+4 \\\\ 2x+2z+w-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92ef9b92e04af929b07ba534c39c4b6bd97125beac6798d6a79c4ac0af55f52d"} {"question" "q-73853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+2y+2z+w-1=0"] ["math" "2x+2z+w-3=0"] ["math" "2x+2z-w-5=0"] ["math" "2x-2y+2z+w-5=0"] ["math" "2x-2z+w+1=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x+2z+w-3=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=\\sqrt{xyz}$ at the point $(2,2,4,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xyz})=\\frac{\\sqrt{yz}}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xyz})=\\frac{\\sqrt{xz}}{2\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xyz})=\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(2,2,4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,2,4) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 4}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,2,4) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 4}}{2\\sqrt{2}}=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,2,4) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2}}{2\\sqrt{4}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-4 & =(x-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(2,2,4)+(y-2)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(2,2,4)+(z-4)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(2,2,4) \\\\ w-4 & =(x-2)+(y-2)+\\frac{1}{2}(z-4) \\\\ w-4 & =x+y+\\frac{z}{2}-6 \\\\ -x-y-\\frac{z}{2}+w+2 & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y+z-2w-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92ef9b92e04af929b07ba534c39c4b6bd97125beac6798d6a79c4ac0af55f52d"} {"question" "q-73854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+2y+z-2w-4=0"] ["math" "2x+2y-z+2w-12=0"] ["math" "2x+2y-z-2w+4=0"] ["math" "2x-2y+2z-2w=0"] ["math" "2x-2y-z+2w-4=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x+2y+z-2w-4=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which points on the surface $z=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨0,2,-2⟩?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨0,2,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨0,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}c\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{cx}{y}+2cy=2 \\\\ -c=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=2$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{1}+2(2)(1)=2\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =(-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-1,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73868" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92ef9b92e04af929b07ba534c39c4b6bd97125beac6798d6a79c4ac0af55f52d"} {"question" "q-73868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,1,1)"] ["math" "(2,1,1)"] ["text" "$(-1,1,1)$ and $(1,-1,1)$"] ["text" "$(-1,1,1)$ and $(2,1,1)$"] ["text" "$(1,-1,1)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-73868" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-1,1,1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-129481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w=z\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)$ at the point $(0,0,3,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-129481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))=\\frac{2xz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))=\\frac{2yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,0,3) & =\\frac{2(0)(3)}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,0,3) & =\\frac{2(0)(3)}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(0,0,3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-0 & =(x-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(0,0,3)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(0,0,3)+(z-3)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(0,0,3) \\\\ w & =0x+0y+0(z-3) \\\\ w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-129481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92ef9b92e04af929b07ba534c39c4b6bd97125beac6798d6a79c4ac0af55f52d"} {"question" "q-129481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-129481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+y-z+w+3=0"] ["math" "w=0"] ["math" "x-2z+w+6=0"] ["math" "x-z-w+3="] ["math" "z+w-3=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-129481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "w=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-129557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "At which point on the surface $z=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v=⟨4,-2,-1⟩?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-129557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨4,-2,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨4,-2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6cx=4 \\\\ 2cy=-2 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6(1)x=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(1)y=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-129557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "92ef9b92e04af929b07ba534c39c4b6bd97125beac6798d6a79c4ac0af55f52d"} {"question" "q-129557" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_sha256" "39e9118b70be7a27436c80254eec4d925329672785dd8e4906b72b51903ee49a"} {"question" "q-129557" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,-1,4)"] ["math" "(-\\frac{1}{3},0,\\frac{1}{9})"] ["math" "(0,1,1)"] ["math" "(1,0,3)"] ["math" "(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071809/state.json" "file_s